    "case_id": "763",
    "case_summary": "A female neonate born at 37+6 weeks gestation with birth weight of 2790g and Apgar score 5-8-9. Required resuscitation after delivery and NICU transfer due to low oxygen saturation. Notable features include:- Inaudible cry and laryngeal stridor- Difficulty swallowing with choking and excessive oral secretions- Hypotonia and flaccid limbs- Mother's history: strabismus, cataract, and cardiac anomalies including PDA- Family history: No siblings, no previous pregnancies\n\n- Respiratory: Laryngeal stridor present- Neurological: Hypotonia, flaccid limbs- Eyes: Does not open eyes spontaneously, corneal opacity present- Facial features: High forehead and small lower jaw developed by 30 days of age- Cardiac: PDA, patent foramen ovale, widened coronary sinus, permanent left superior vena cava and pericardial effusion\n\nLaboratory Tests:- CSF: Mildly elevated WBC count initially, normalized on repeat testing- Blood strand mass spectrometry: No abnormalities- Urine gas chromatography: Normal- Electrolytes: Normal range- Plasma lactate: 1.6-2.2 mmol/LImaging Studies:- Neck and larynx CT: Normal findings- Chest CT: Shows pneumonia- Cranial CT + enhancement: Slightly hypointense shadows with poorly defined borders in bilateral frontoparieto-occipitotemporal lobes and parietal ventriclesOther Tests:- Initial EEG: Normal- Follow-up EEG: Increased amplitude of all-conductor waves with scattered spikes- Fiber-optic bronchoscopy: Pronounced swelling in bilateral arytenoid epiglottis folds- Genetic testing: Heterozygous variant in ATAD3A gene (c.1517A>C; p.Q506P)",
